## What to deliver

Build and rig the Pump up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches the
brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and the
rig is complete: a broad brass frame carrying a great iron rocking beam pivoted at its
center, weighted by a counterweight at one end and driving a piston rod at the other, a long
intake
snorkel reaching out to the side, a flywheel on the frame, and a verdigris accent on the
counterweight and the flywheel rim sculpted **only** in `#2f7d72` and appearing nowhere else
on the model. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim`
animation subcommands: an **`idle`** (a slow self-playing loop that turns the flywheel and
holds the beam nearly level while the engine ticks over) and a **`pump`** (a heavy loop that
rocks the beam through its full stroke, drives the piston rod and counterweight, spins the
flywheel fast, and reads unmistakably as working distinct from the idle). The animations and
the accent region are the fixed contract — produce all of them; the parts and joints that
realize them are yours. You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top, but
do not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `pump`. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim
render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered
part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an
animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and
`rig.json` are your finished submission.
